WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.670
[LAUREL/YANNI CLIP PLAYS]

00:00:07.502 --> 00:00:08.460
DANA BOEBINGER: Laurel.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.420
KEVIN SITEK: Yanny.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.485
DANA BOEBINGER:
It just is Laurel.

00:00:12.485 --> 00:00:14.860
KEVIN SITEK: I've listened to
this so many different ways

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.100
and all I can ever
hear is Yanny.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.930
DANA BOEBINGER: So this is kind
of like the auditory version

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.030
of the dress that we
saw a few years ago

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:25.330
on the internet, in that its
a physical stimulus that's

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:27.400
out there and it's
kind of ambiguous,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.920
and depending on how
the brain is choosing

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.190
to interpret the
signal, you're going

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.000
to perceive it a
totally different way.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.540
So here, we're looking
at a spectrogram

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.880
of the auditory
illusion itself--

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.100
the Laurel or Yanny word.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.350
And what we're actually
seeing is that, on the x-axis,

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.750
in the horizontal
direction, is time.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.970
And then the y-axis
is the frequency.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:51.949
So it's low at bottom
and high at the top.

00:00:51.949 --> 00:00:54.490
And you can see that, while it
sounds like a voice might only

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:57.100
be one pitch, there's
actually a whole bunch

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.060
of different frequencies
included in that speech sound.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.550
KEVIN SITEK: There's a
big gap on the lower side.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:04.690
It's not white.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.470
So there's definitely
some energy there--

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.090
some acoustic energy.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:13.210
And so what could be
happening is that people

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:18.880
who hear Laurel are actually
putting another line

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.010
through that space.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.860
Whereas people who
hear Yanny, they're

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.220
going to say that whatever is
in between the two really dark

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.032
bands is actually just noise.

00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:30.490
DANA BOEBINGER:
Yeah, so it all has

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.590
to do with what frequencies
your brain is deciding

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.350
are important for figuring
out what speech sound it is.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.210
KEVIN SITEK: So this
is what it sounds

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:38.890
like if you cut out
the high frequencies

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.580
and only have the
low frequencies.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.220
[LAUREL/YANNI CLIP PLAYS]

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.100
I even hear Laurel.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:43.750
SPEAKER 1: I heard Laurel.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:44.020
SPEAKER 2: Mm-hmm.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:44.440
It's Laurel.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:44.830
DANA BOEBINGER:
Yeah, that's Laurel.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:45.040
SPEAKER 2: It's Laurel.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:45.350
KEVIN SITEK: Right.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:46.300
Definitely.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.570
And if you cut out the
low frequencies and you

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.301
only have a high frequencies--

00:01:51.301 --> 00:01:54.007
[LAUREL/YANNI CLIP PLAYS]

00:01:54.007 --> 00:01:54.682
Was that Yanny?

00:01:54.682 --> 00:01:55.890
DANA BOEBINGER: That's Yanny.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.306
KEVIN SITEK: I
think it's probably

00:01:57.306 --> 00:02:00.040
good to have some friends
on the Laurel side.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.248
DANA BOEBINGER: That's right.

00:02:01.248 --> 00:02:03.200
We all need friends
in Laurel places.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.150
[LAUGHS]

